{"decision":"RECORDED AS BRAINSTORMING, NOT AS A PROPOSAL. @ajs, 2026-10-14: "for reflow2, I was thinking about a new idea for things to design. One could be designing experiments, or designing a research paper (thesis or dissertation, for example), or even designing a legal case. All these share the kind of the same idea of "conveying thought" in a logical, coherent manner. ... it is much more abstract than what we've done in the past, but I feel that the same basic principles of what are the ideas, constraints, requirements, etc that you could use a graph to describe."

WHAT IS ALREADY SETTLED, so this is a HOW, not a WHETHER:
- req:design-anything (accepted): "Software, hardware, a document, a process — the vocabulary stays neutral." A reasoned document is inside it.
- dec:idea-reflow2-does-fewer-things-and-does-them-really-well (settled 2026-10-13). Job 1 is "the one shared record of who decided what and why, across models, people and agents". An argument is exactly that record. Job 3 is "checks that don't reason".
- dec:idea-a-domain-seed-template-starts-a-design-in-the-fields-own-shape was REJECTED 2026-10-13: genesis takes no domain seed template. Nothing below may start a design pre-filled with an argument's shape.
- It is a concrete case of the open dec:idea-reflow2-serves-a-project-that-is-not-software (the vocabulary is neutral, and the delivery is still software-shaped).

HOW THE PARTS MAP onto what exists (a sketch to test, not a rule):
- AN ARGUMENT FOR AND AGAINST:
  - the claim to establish → a Requirement ("what must be shown");
  - premises and assumptions → Constraints;
  - sources and evidence → Artifacts (the cited text) and dated findings;
  - competing readings → a Decision with its alternatives;
  - objections and replies → CONTRADICTS, RISKS and MITIGATES, each with its evidence;
  - "is this claim supported?" → the thread from claim to cited evidence.
- AN EXPERIMENT:
  - hypotheses → Requirements;
  - the measurement that would confirm or refute each → a Verification, written BEFORE the result, as in preregistration; the design already dates what was decided when;
  - controls → Constraints;
  - threats to validity → RISKS;
  - sample size and statistics → flo2-calc's kept record.
- A RESEARCH PAPER, THESIS OR DISSERTATION:
  - research questions → Requirements;
  - chapters → parts, each allocated the claims it must establish;
  - the literature → cited Artifacts;
  - the thread shows any claim no chapter establishes and any chapter that establishes nothing.
- A LEGAL CASE:
  - each element that must be proven → a Requirement;
  - evidence for each → Artifacts and Verifications;
  - defences → CONTRADICTS;
  - precedent → cited sources;
  - the burden of proof is the threshold for when an element counts as shown.

OPTIONS, none chosen:
(a) NOTHING NEW IN THE ENGINE. The neutral vocabulary as it is, plus the mapping above written down where an agent reads it.
(b) A SERVED SKILL PER KIND (design-an-argument, design-an-experiment, …) that walks the interview and uses (a)'s mapping, as flo2's design-a-physical-piece does for objects. It shapes the conversation, never the starting graph, which keeps it clear of the rejected seed template.
(c) THE PERSON'S OWN WORDS on the screen: claim, objection, evidence, hypothesis, element, instead of requirement and constraint. The design already records what a person calls a node (req:a-node-records-what-the-user-calls-it, delivered).
(d) NEW NODE AND EDGE TYPES for argumentation (Claim, Premise, Warrant, Objection, after Toulmin).
(e) A READER'S OUTPUT: the argument, experiment plan or case written up as a document for a reader, each sentence tied to its source.

PUSHBACK, RECORDED BESIDE IT:
- THE FIT IS HISTORICAL, NOT A STRETCH. Recording design decisions with their issues, positions and arguments began as argument mapping: Rittel's IBIS, around 1970, for "wicked problems". reflow2's Decision-with-alternatives-and-rationale is a descendant. So (a) to (c) are likely enough, and (d) is likely not needed.
- (d) fails the "fewer things" yardstick. It adds schema for one kind of design against a vocabulary that is meant to stay neutral.
- STRUCTURE IS NOT TRUTH (job 3). The graph can show that every claim cites evidence and every objection has a reply. It cannot show the argument is sound. A well-connected argument graph can READ as a proven case. Whatever is built must say what the checks do and do not mean.
- TWO SIDES MAY NEVER CONVERGE. An engineering design ends in one built thing; an argument "for and against" deliberately keeps both. "Delivered" assumes one side wins. Holding two sides with no verdict needs a way to say "contested, by design", or the counts mislead.
- A LEGAL CASE carries extra weight:
  - it must never present as legal advice;
  - confidential case material on a hosted service raises privilege questions;
  - an AI agent inventing a case citation is a known, sanctioned failure in court filings. As with flo2-calc's "never invent a constant", a citation must come from a cited source, never the model's memory.

SIZE, PLAINLY (a finding):
- (a): writing only.
- (b): small per skill.
- (c): already delivered; showing it is small.
- (e): moderate, and close to flo2's reader's-document work.
- (d): large, and a schema change.","kind":"exploratory","name":"OPEN — how does reflow2 design a line of reasoning meant to convey thought (an argument for and against, an experiment, a research paper or thesis, a legal case) with the vocabulary it already has?","rationale":"A new class of design people would bring, raised with a real example the same day (Chris's question, recorded separately as a possible first trial). Recorded with the settled intent it sits inside, so a later reader doesn't mistake it for an open question of scope. Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019Xbw6PG9mXJiU2Sb421fnq","status":"proposed"}
